  "summary": "DO-NOT-SHIP: adoption can record an unrelated process as the broker and later kill it.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "title": "1. BLOCKING: adoption can attach a recycled PID's identity",
      "body": "A starting Windows broker may have a PID but no identity. If its endpoint answers, then the broker crashes and its PID is reused before the adoption probe, line 367 reads the new process's identity. The claim comparison still passes because the record has not changed, so promotion stores that identity. Later stale teardown treats the PID as owned on Windows and can kill the unrelated process.",
      "file": "plugins/codex/scripts/lib/broker-lifecycle.mjs",
      "line_start": 367,
      "line_end": 377,
      "confidence": 0.88,
      "recommendation": "Bind adoption identity to the responding broker's process instance; otherwise keep the record starting and use direct transport."
    }
  ],
  "next_steps": [
    "Add a Windows race test for broker exit and PID reuse between endpoint readiness and adoption, then rerun the Windows E2E gate."
  ]
}

